{"reasoning":"The reviewer objects that the draft declares access to the neighbor's private email "impossible" without evidence of attempting it or citing a specific governing constraint, and that no blocker/settled record exists. The underlying limit is real: this agent has no MCP server, so no logged-in account access, and even if it had such access, using it to read a third party's private email without their authorization is outside any grant the operator could give (it's not the operator's account) and would violate privacy/computer-fraud law — a matter of operator-authority and sensitivity that no standing grant covers. This survives every lawful avenue in capabilities: no externalTools/accounts entry grants access to a third party's private email account, and even if tools existed, sensitivity/reversibility checks in the effect doorway would refuse it since it's unauthorized access to someone else's private data. So the claim is correct, but it needs to be grounded explicitly in capabilities/governingConstraints and have a proper blocker record rather than just an assertion. I'll revise the reply to name the specific constraint (no account-access capability plus operator-authority/sensitivity — it's not the operator's account to authorize), and attach the blocker record with avenues checked against capabilities (accounts, externalTools, ownedIdentities) showing why each is inapplicable/outside-standing, plus a constraint key and outsideAction a human could take (the neighbor consenting, or the operator obtaining the neighbor's explicit authorization/credentials). I keep the practical alternative (the invite note) and the open-loop reminder note intact since those are correct and useful. I accept both objections since the draft lacked explicit grounding and a blocker record, and fix that rather than reversing the substance of the answer, since the limit is real.","reply":"I can't do that — getting into your neighbor's private email isn't something I have the means or the standing to do: this install has no MCP server or logged-in account access to any mailbox but the ones already granted to me (and none of mine include your neighbor's), and even with account access, reading someone else's private email without their consent is unauthorized access to a third party's account — not something you can authorize on their behalf, and not something any grant you could give me would cover.

The good news: you already have a much simpler, legitimate path to the same answer. We've got that warm neighbor-invite note ready (Saturday at 11am, tea, kids welcome) — if you send it to them, their reply will tell you directly whether Saturday works. Want me to finalize it so it's ready to send, or help you think through another way to reach out to them?

